00:47:38.120 --> 00:47:40.740
And I asked Steve Burgess, how did we clear that?

1002
00:47:40.880 --> 00:47:46.480
Remember when we did that episode, gosh, what season is it, where we do all the flashbacks?

1003
00:47:46.640 --> 00:47:46.980
Mm-hmm.

1004
00:47:47.160 --> 00:47:53.300
And we found out that every time you used a clip from an old episode, you had to pay the

1005
00:47:53.300 --> 00:47:55.560
director who shot that footage a fee?

1006
00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:58.800
I was like, how did we put this little promo together?

1007
00:47:58.800 --> 00:48:00.260
It must have been so expensive.

1008
00:48:01.040 --> 00:48:07.880
So Steve Burgess said, because the clips were from the office, airing on the office, we didn't

1009
00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:14.060
have to get any special clearance from anyone, but we did have to pay anyone who was not in

1010
00:48:14.060 --> 00:48:18.780
the episode, mostly Steve, a SAG scale payment.

1011
00:48:18.780 --> 00:48:25.760
And the DGA, which is the Directors Guild, and the WGA, the Writers Guild, have a formula for how

1012
00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:29.900
they would pay each of the writers or directors to use their clip.

1013
00:48:30.560 --> 00:48:35.140
And he said he doesn't know how much it cost ultimately, but it was a lot of paperwork.

1014
00:48:35.140 --> 00:48:36.140
I bet.

1015
00:48:36.520 --> 00:48:36.720
Mm-hmm.

1016
00:48:37.860 --> 00:48:42.040
Andy seems to be the only one that is just giddy that all this footage is going online.

1017
00:48:42.220 --> 00:48:43.780
He says, we're killing it on the internet.

1018
00:48:44.680 --> 00:48:46.000
Angela needs to go for a walk.

1019
00:48:46.180 --> 00:48:47.340
She needs to think about things.

1020
00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:48.620
Everyone else agrees.

1021
00:48:48.860 --> 00:48:50.580
And they sneak away to discuss.

1022
00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:53.180
I also remember doing this scene.

1023
00:48:53.380 --> 00:48:54.200
In the warehouse?

1024
00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:55.660
Yeah, I so remember it.

1025
00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:56.100
I do too.

1026
00:48:56.100 --> 00:49:01.000
They all turn off their mics, and they're like, okay, what exactly do we think the crew

1027
00:49:01.000 --> 00:49:01.800
got on tape?

1028
00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:07.660
And Pete suggests, you know what, Pam, you should reach out to Brian to find out.

1029
00:49:08.060 --> 00:49:10.100
I mean, she knows Brian.

1030
00:49:10.360 --> 00:49:12.320
Maybe she could get some information for them.

1031
00:49:12.500 --> 00:49:16.680
Yeah, they want to know exactly how much of our private lives are going to be exposed

1032
00:49:16.680 --> 00:49:18.060
when this documentary airs.

1033
00:49:18.360 --> 00:49:24.000
Well, there are two people who are not in this meeting in the warehouse, and they are Clark

1034
00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:25.340
and Dwight.

1035
00:49:25.340 --> 00:49:30.180
And that is because they're out in the parking lot along with a giant tractor.

1036
00:49:30.760 --> 00:49:34.240
And Mr. Brueger is trying to propose a 60-40 split.

1037
00:49:34.420 --> 00:49:36.200
Dwight wants a 50-50 split.

1038
00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:39.400
Some of Esther's sisters are flirting with Clark.

1039
00:49:39.860 --> 00:49:44.000
I should let you know that Mr. Brueger was played by Alan Harvey.

1040
00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:48.560
He has had recurring roles on Billions, The Man in the High Castle, and Mad Men.

1041
00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:53.540
He is a stand-up comedian who was, incidentally, born in my hometown of St. Louis, Missouri.

1042
00:49:53.540 --> 00:49:59.080
So there were two St. Louisans in this episode, Alan and Ryan Howard, I guess, and me, three.

1043
00:49:59.360 --> 00:49:59.900
And Phyllis.

1044
00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:00.720
Four.

1045
00:50:00.900 --> 00:50:01.260
And Ellie.

1046
00:50:01.840 --> 00:50:02.180
Five.

1047
00:50:02.780 --> 00:50:03.820
Oh, my goodness.

1048
00:50:04.700 --> 00:50:05.720
Are there any more?

1049
00:50:05.880 --> 00:50:10.620
And if Ken Kwap has directed any of those promo spots, he's sort of represented as well.

1050
00:50:10.620 --> 00:50:13.880
Wow, St. Louis coming hard for this episode.

1051
00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:15.640
I got curious.

1052
00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:18.460
What is the cost of a new tractor?

1053
00:50:18.780 --> 00:50:20.440
What are we talking about here?

1054
00:50:20.620 --> 00:50:23.340
What is this contract that Dwight is entering into?

1055
00:50:23.540 --> 00:50:26.320
What's he going to be in for if he signs this deal?

1056
00:50:26.500 --> 00:50:27.680
They're very expensive.

1057
00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:29.120
I figured.

1058
00:50:29.120 --> 00:50:34.000
I went to an article from Victoria Farm Equipment.

1059
00:50:34.660 --> 00:50:39.020
It's called The Ultimate Guide to Understanding How Much Does a Tractor Cost?

1060
00:50:39.520 --> 00:50:40.720
I wanted to know.

1061
00:50:41.440 --> 00:50:47.200
It said that the cost of a new tractor can vary greatly based on its size, power, and features.

1062
00:50:47.480 --> 00:50:49.360
Am I telling you things you already know, Angela?

1063
00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:51.700
Well, I would go with my dad.

1064
00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:55.760
One of his favorite things to do was to go see what new tractors were out there.

1065
00:50:55.940 --> 00:50:57.020
He had two.

1066
00:50:57.660 --> 00:51:05.180
One was a really, really big Case International tractor, and the other one was a smaller blue Ford tractor.

1067
00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:10.760
And then he had a baler, a hay baler, and he had a front-end loader.

1068
00:51:11.380 --> 00:51:19.620
Now, when you say he liked to see the new tractors, is this sort of like how maybe you see like an open house sign in your neighborhood and you just want to go looky-loo?

1069
00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:20.920
You're not going to go buy it.

1070
00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:23.060
You just want to see what's the lateSt.

1071
00:51:23.300 --> 00:51:24.140
What are people doing?

1072
00:51:24.360 --> 00:51:24.740
I love that.

1073
00:51:24.740 --> 00:51:25.620
What are the new gadgets?

1074
00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:33.780
Well, I guess larger tractors with an engine capacity greater than 100 horsepower can range between $50,000 and $80,000.

1075
00:51:34.520 --> 00:51:38.800
Mid-sized tractors are more like $25,000 to $50,000.

1076
00:51:39.320 --> 00:51:42.220
But then, like you said, there's all these attachments.

1077
00:51:42.960 --> 00:51:49.040
So to really utilize a tractor's potential, you're going to want to equip it with different attachments.

1078
00:51:49.040 --> 00:51:51.440
And each one of those attachments is more money.

1079
00:51:51.560 --> 00:51:57.520
So all of this is to say, yeah, this is a big purchase.

1080
00:51:57.800 --> 00:51:58.000
Yeah.

1081
00:51:58.540 --> 00:52:04.460
Not only money-wise, but like in the future, maintaining the tractor and everything.

1082
00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:10.680
Well, Esther's sisters have kind of all gathered around Clark, and he's really enjoying the attention.

1083
00:52:10.680 --> 00:52:14.740
And then one of them says, you know, you would be a great one to buy an auger with.

1084
00:52:15.720 --> 00:52:17.740
And the wheels start turning for Clark.

1085
00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:18.660
Yeah.

1086
00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:24.020
He goes over and interrupts Dwight, and he's like, dude, we're being conned.

1087
00:52:24.220 --> 00:52:24.380
Yeah.

1088
00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:27.140
No one this hot would date us.

1089
00:52:27.300 --> 00:52:28.200
They want something.

1090
00:52:28.660 --> 00:52:28.800
Yeah.

1091
00:52:29.500 --> 00:52:31.560
And Dwight's like, no, I don't think so.

1092
00:52:31.700 --> 00:52:36.240
And then he says, she just said that I should buy an auger with her.

1093
00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:38.600
And he doesn't even know what an auger is.

1094
00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:41.180
And Dwight is like, oh, my gosh.

1095
00:52:41.280 --> 00:52:44.960
No woman would want a man who doesn't know what an auger is.

1096
00:52:45.660 --> 00:52:46.060
Yeah.

1097
00:52:46.220 --> 00:52:49.960
And he tells Esther's dad, I'm going to need a minute to think about this.

1098
00:52:50.200 --> 00:52:50.580
Mm-hmm.

1099
00:52:51.140 --> 00:52:53.480
Well, let me give you a quick guest star breakdown.

1100
00:52:54.020 --> 00:52:56.480
Esther's sister, Bertha, was played by Kelly Daly.

1101
00:52:56.780 --> 00:52:59.280
Her sister, Edna, was played by Kamala Jones.

1102
00:52:59.540 --> 00:53:02.820
And then there was a nameless sister played by Wendy McComb.

1103
00:53:03.100 --> 00:53:06.240
And the tractor seller was Richard Augustine.

1104
00:53:06.440 --> 00:53:10.180
Well, back in the offices, Stanley's doing damage control.

1105
00:53:10.480 --> 00:53:13.420
He calls his wife and says, hey, you know what?

1106
00:53:13.480 --> 00:53:16.740
The TV repairman said, we need to keep our TV off for a couple of months.

1107
00:53:16.900 --> 00:53:18.000
There's a wiring issue.

1108
00:53:18.620 --> 00:53:20.860
That's the best excuse he could come up with.

1109
00:53:21.040 --> 00:53:22.000
It's not great.

1110
00:53:22.240 --> 00:53:22.500
No.

1111
00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:26.100
Andy is becoming obsessed with the comment section.

1112
00:53:26.360 --> 00:53:28.940
And some of the comments are snarky.

1113
00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:30.280
And he starts responding.

1114
00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:31.260
Yes.

1115
00:53:31.520 --> 00:53:33.380
This is a slippery slope, Andy.

1116
00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:33.900
Be careful.

1117
00:53:34.600 --> 00:53:36.780
Meanwhile, in the secret stairwell.

1118
00:53:37.020 --> 00:53:38.580
The super secret stairwell.

1119
00:53:38.680 --> 00:53:39.040
Mm-hmm.

1120
00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:45.000
Angela asks Oscar, how much does the documentary crew know about you and the senator?

1121
00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:47.660
This scene was so much fun to film.

1122
00:53:47.660 --> 00:53:51.220
You guys, we improvised the whole second half of it.

1123
00:53:51.420 --> 00:53:55.840
The part we did as scripted was Oscar says, well, they caught us kissing on Halloween.

1124
00:53:56.060 --> 00:53:58.580
And Angela goes, he was dressed as Reagan.

1125
00:53:58.580 --> 00:53:59.860
And she slaps him.

1126
00:53:59.940 --> 00:54:02.660
And then Oscar says, well, he kissed like Jack Kennedy.

1127
00:54:02.800 --> 00:54:04.960
And she's like, and then she slaps him again.

1128
00:54:04.960 --> 00:54:06.500
I improvised that next slap.

1129
00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:09.840
And then he's like, stop slapping me.

1130
00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:11.240
And I'm like, stop kissing him.

1131
00:54:11.540 --> 00:54:13.300
All of that, we started improvising.

1132
00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:18.260
And basically, we didn't get back on script till the part where I say, well, you call him.

1133
00:54:18.340 --> 00:54:19.140
No, you call him.

1134
00:54:19.140 --> 00:54:24.080
Now everyone has this awareness that the documentary crew could be filming or listening in at any time.

1135
00:54:24.260 --> 00:54:30.000
So during this whole exchange, Angela, you are playing Lee Greenwood's God Bless the USA on an old boombox.

1136
00:54:30.180 --> 00:54:31.140
That was scripted.

1137
00:54:31.300 --> 00:54:31.680
Yes.

1138
00:54:31.900 --> 00:54:33.340
I asked Steve Burgess about it.

1139
00:54:33.440 --> 00:54:35.920
He said it was one of our more expensive music purchases.

1140
00:54:36.120 --> 00:54:36.520
Oh, wow.

1141
00:54:36.860 --> 00:54:37.920
$35,000.

1142
00:54:38.340 --> 00:54:38.600
Wow.

1143
00:54:38.840 --> 00:54:39.420
Mm-hmm.

1144
00:54:39.980 --> 00:54:48.520
Well, Pam has decided she will, for the sake of everyone at the office, go and find out from Brian what footage they have.

1145
00:54:48.640 --> 00:54:48.920
Yes.

1146
00:54:49.660 --> 00:54:54.400
You see her walking down the street, and she's kind of looking around like, did I sneak away?

1147
00:54:54.680 --> 00:54:55.440
Did I get away?

1148
00:54:55.580 --> 00:54:57.020
And she doesn't see a camera.

1149
00:54:57.280 --> 00:55:00.400
She gets to Brian's house, and it is a mess.

1150
00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:01.680
He's a bachelor now.

1151
00:55:02.060 --> 00:55:03.520
He's such a mess.

1152
00:55:03.680 --> 00:55:04.860
You see moving boxes.

1153
00:55:05.300 --> 00:55:06.340
And he's embarrassed.

1154
00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:07.020
He's like, sorry.

1155
00:55:07.300 --> 00:55:08.860
He's like, let's go out to the balcony.

1156
00:55:09.020 --> 00:55:10.140
He's going to get them some beers.

1157
00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:11.980
Well, I remember this location.

1158
00:55:11.980 --> 00:55:14.340
It was a real apartment complex in Woodland Hills.

1159
00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:18.300
And all of this thing about them sitting on the balcony and his place being a mess,

1160
00:55:18.300 --> 00:55:21.480
that was so that we could draw them outside.

1161
00:55:21.780 --> 00:55:24.600
So that the audio could pick them up hypothetically.

1162
00:55:24.940 --> 00:55:25.060
Right.

1163
00:55:25.160 --> 00:55:27.100
That the documentary crew could capture this.

1164
00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:27.780
Yeah.

1165
00:55:27.960 --> 00:55:30.360
And they're going to have this really intimate conversation.

1166
00:55:30.360 --> 00:55:36.020
And I remember shooting it, and it felt really intimate because we couldn't see the cameras

1167
00:55:36.020 --> 00:55:37.240
like we usually do.

1168
00:55:37.540 --> 00:55:38.240
You know?

1169
00:55:38.440 --> 00:55:40.940
It felt like two people just having a conversation.

1170
00:55:41.440 --> 00:55:44.980
I did find it very interesting that he went and got them beers.

1171
00:55:45.240 --> 00:55:47.440
I'm like, all right, she's got to go back to work.

1172
00:55:47.580 --> 00:55:48.440
This is day drinking.

1173
00:55:48.860 --> 00:55:49.980
What is happening?

1174
00:55:50.120 --> 00:55:50.540
I know.

1175
00:55:50.800 --> 00:55:53.040
At this man's home.

1176
00:55:53.220 --> 00:55:54.440
I was like, wait, what?

1177
00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:57.360
I kind of got the sense that's all there was.

1178
00:55:57.620 --> 00:55:58.000
Yeah.

1179
00:55:58.160 --> 00:55:58.740
You know what I mean?

1180
00:55:58.780 --> 00:55:59.040
Right.

1181
00:55:59.040 --> 00:56:01.200
That's what's in the fridge at this guy's place.

1182
00:56:01.200 --> 00:56:06.560
But Pam kind of ends up blurting out, do you think Jim has changed?

1183
00:56:07.160 --> 00:56:08.200
Am I crazy?

1184
00:56:08.940 --> 00:56:11.780
And he says, no, you're not crazy.

1185
00:56:12.280 --> 00:56:17.780
There's a line we took out here where Brian was going to say, I mean, if you want, I can

1186
00:56:17.780 --> 00:56:19.460
show you Jim's theme sheet.

1187
00:56:20.180 --> 00:56:20.380
Yeah.

1188
00:56:21.340 --> 00:56:23.400
And Pam's like, what's a theme sheet?

1189
00:56:23.660 --> 00:56:26.860
He's like, well, we kind of pick a theme for each person in the office.

1190
00:56:26.860 --> 00:56:32.440
And then we are kind of told to try and capture that because that's what they're tending to

1191
00:56:32.440 --> 00:56:32.800
do.

1192
00:56:33.580 --> 00:56:36.520
And she's like, oh, what's Jim's theme?

1193
00:56:36.720 --> 00:56:39.380
And he's like, Jim's theme right now is selfish.

1194
00:56:40.520 --> 00:56:45.840
So the documentary crew would then try to capture any moment where they thought Jim was

1195
00:56:45.840 --> 00:56:46.640
being selfish.

1196
00:56:46.880 --> 00:56:47.400
Yeah.

1197
00:56:47.560 --> 00:56:49.180
And tell that side of the story.

1198
00:56:49.500 --> 00:56:49.840
Right.

1199
00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:51.800
We ended up taking that out.

1200
00:56:51.800 --> 00:56:58.200
We felt like that made the documentary crew seem a little bit less like a documentary crew

1201
00:56:58.200 --> 00:57:01.040
and actually more like a reality TV crew.

1202
00:57:01.820 --> 00:57:03.340
That's not really documentary.

1203
00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:03.920
Yeah.

1204
00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:07.580
But anyway, he does say, no, Pam, you're not crazy.

1205
00:57:07.700 --> 00:57:09.380
And that kind of makes her feel better.

1206
00:57:09.560 --> 00:57:10.820
And then she asks him.

1207
00:57:11.260 --> 00:57:13.000
Makes her feel better, but doesn't.

1208
00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:14.180
Yeah, that's true.

1209
00:57:14.840 --> 00:57:19.320
Then she starts saying, OK, well, the reason I'm here is because of this promo footage

1210
00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:23.300
and people really are freaking out, how did that work?

1211
00:57:23.580 --> 00:57:25.180
Like, how much stuff did you really get?

1212
00:57:25.480 --> 00:57:28.240
And this is when Brian says to her, you know what?

1213
00:57:28.400 --> 00:57:30.000
We pretty much got everything.

1214
00:57:30.540 --> 00:57:33.640
And she's like, well, what if we turned our mic packs off?

1215
00:57:33.720 --> 00:57:38.400
And he says, well, the parabolic mics can pick up sound 100 yards away.

1216
00:57:38.700 --> 00:57:41.560
So yeah, they got everything.

1217
00:57:42.520 --> 00:57:44.620
And this really upsets Pam.

1218
00:57:44.620 --> 00:57:48.880
She feels betrayed and she leaves.

1219
00:57:49.080 --> 00:57:49.820
She's upset.

1220
00:57:50.840 --> 00:57:51.020
Yeah.

1221
00:57:51.980 --> 00:57:57.480
Jen Salata sent in an amazing audio clip breaking down this whole scene from a director's perspective.

1222
00:57:57.700 --> 00:57:59.080
And we want you to hear it.

1223
00:57:59.420 --> 00:58:02.800
I definitely remember there were a lot of discussions about the Pam Bryan scene.

1224
00:58:03.220 --> 00:58:10.180
From a directing point of view, what I remember is a lot of thinking went into making it very

1225
00:58:10.180 --> 00:58:15.180
clear that it was a spy shot and there was foliage featured in the foreground of the shot just

1226
00:58:15.180 --> 00:58:18.080
to make it extra clear for two reasons.

1227
00:58:18.260 --> 00:58:23.480
One, that I don't think that Pam would have been that vulnerable, surely, if she knew that

1228
00:58:23.480 --> 00:58:24.960
there was any chance of a camera being there.

1229
00:58:25.120 --> 00:58:30.300
And two, this was an episode where everybody was like hyper-focused that the camera crew caught

1230
00:58:30.300 --> 00:58:32.800
things on camera that they didn't realize were on camera.

1231
00:58:32.800 --> 00:58:37.820
So it was really important to shoot it from further away.

1232
00:58:37.820 --> 00:58:45.100
And what that meant was sacrificing a few things like his look at the door when she comes to

1233
00:58:45.100 --> 00:58:45.620
see him.

1234
00:58:48.080 --> 00:58:55.180
And we ended up not seeing Brian's side for the stuff at the door, but we were able to

1235
00:58:55.180 --> 00:58:59.460
focus and highlight Pam walking behind him, being out there on the balcony for a second

1236
00:58:59.460 --> 00:59:05.920
by herself and really chose to focus on Pam throughout that scene, not using the two shot

1237
00:59:05.920 --> 00:59:07.560
as much as the shot on Pam.

1238
00:59:07.700 --> 00:59:13.900
Those little flips, like the vulnerability and the feeling like, I want to ask this guy

1239
00:59:13.900 --> 00:59:15.160
if he noticed anything.

1240
00:59:15.440 --> 00:59:19.860
I usually talk to Jim about things and I'm feeling distant from Jim right now and I just

1241
00:59:19.860 --> 00:59:20.920
impulsively want to ask.

1242
00:59:21.080 --> 00:59:24.840
And then sort of realizing that she asked and pulling back a little and feeling a little

1243
00:59:24.840 --> 00:59:25.380
bit exposed.

1244
00:59:25.380 --> 00:59:29.820
And then on top of that, asking the question about the cameras and the privacy and how

1245
00:59:29.820 --> 00:59:36.480
much they got on tape and that feeling of now on top of feeling a little bit exposed, hearing

1246
00:59:36.480 --> 00:59:37.120
that news.

1247
00:59:37.120 --> 00:59:43.420
I just love that scene and how it builds and I love Jenna's performance and I just thought

1248
00:59:43.420 --> 00:59:45.440
they did such a great job with that scene.

1249
00:59:47.720 --> 00:59:49.020
Oh, that's so nice.

1250
00:59:49.220 --> 00:59:49.520
I know.

1251
00:59:49.860 --> 00:59:55.340
I was so glad that Jen was our director because I just trusted her so much.

1252
00:59:55.340 --> 00:59:59.480
She handled relationships for the character so well.

1253
01:00:00.100 --> 01:00:02.360
We got a fan question from Maddie S.

1254
01:00:02.520 --> 01:00:05.400
in Russellville, Arkansas, who said, Jenna, how did you feel

