1314
01:03:10.960 --> 01:03:11.040
Right, right.

1315
01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:12.260
Not because he hit on her.

1316
01:03:12.400 --> 01:03:12.580
Right.

1317
01:03:12.660 --> 01:03:13.820
It's a betrayal of friendship.

1318
01:03:14.140 --> 01:03:15.460
These were her good friends.

1319
01:03:15.660 --> 01:03:18.560
I mean, they would have dinner with him and his wife.

1320
01:03:18.720 --> 01:03:20.380
Like, she trusted him.

1321
01:03:20.580 --> 01:03:20.920
Yes.

1322
01:03:21.080 --> 01:03:21.980
And I get it.

1323
01:03:21.980 --> 01:03:26.240
But, I mean, all this time, he's been hiding this information.

1324
01:03:26.240 --> 01:03:30.480
He's been putting this job ahead of their friendship, and he didn't want to be like,

1325
01:03:30.620 --> 01:03:31.860
hey, warning.

1326
01:03:32.220 --> 01:03:32.540
Yeah.

1327
01:03:32.880 --> 01:03:34.240
Don't go to the stairwell.

1328
01:03:34.240 --> 01:03:38.380
Never have all of those conversations in the stairwell.

1329
01:03:38.640 --> 01:03:39.980
We're filming them.

1330
01:03:40.300 --> 01:03:40.780
Exactly.

1331
01:03:41.440 --> 01:03:42.520
I know.

1332
01:03:43.080 --> 01:03:46.820
And now, also, the fact that these promos have come out,

1333
01:03:47.040 --> 01:03:52.580
you didn't want to take some initiative and be like, hey, you might be wondering.

1334
01:03:52.900 --> 01:03:53.880
Here's the deal.

1335
01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:54.540
Yeah.

1336
01:03:54.980 --> 01:03:57.580
So that's how that all went down.

1337
01:03:57.580 --> 01:04:02.240
But I will tell you this, unlike some of our other days where we were really finding

1338
01:04:02.240 --> 01:04:08.260
the storyline in the moment, this was set and ready to go for when we shot, and it was

1339
01:04:08.260 --> 01:04:12.380
my favorite day of shooting with Chris Diamatopoulos.

1340
01:04:12.680 --> 01:04:19.800
It was just, he is such a beautiful actor, and this was my favorite day, and I got them

1341
01:04:19.800 --> 01:04:20.480
all to myself.

1342
01:04:20.660 --> 01:04:22.200
It was a two-person scene.

1343
01:04:22.320 --> 01:04:25.060
Two-person scenes are so fun because you really get to play.

1344
01:04:25.100 --> 01:04:27.280
That's how I felt about my scenes with Oscar.

1345
01:04:27.580 --> 01:04:27.900
Mm-hmm.

1346
01:04:28.060 --> 01:04:30.000
You have a real partner.

1347
01:04:30.280 --> 01:04:30.520
Yes.

1348
01:04:30.720 --> 01:04:36.300
And the way this was set up, it really just felt like this private moment, and I loved

1349
01:04:36.300 --> 01:04:36.540
it.

1350
01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:42.340
Let's go check in over at Athlead because Jim and Daryl and Ryan are now in the middle

1351
01:04:42.340 --> 01:04:44.220
of reading Ryan's screenplay.

1352
01:04:44.440 --> 01:04:44.800
Mm-hmm.

1353
01:04:45.080 --> 01:04:46.740
And, you know, Jim is trying to wrap this up.

1354
01:04:46.840 --> 01:04:47.340
He's like, you know what?

1355
01:04:47.340 --> 01:04:50.700
Maybe we should finish the rest of this later because this is a big script.

1356
01:04:50.800 --> 01:04:51.700
And Ryan's like, no, no, no.

1357
01:04:52.100 --> 01:04:53.060
Let's keep reading.

1358
01:04:53.580 --> 01:04:56.480
Also, I'm going to need you to get the rights to Darth Vader.

1359
01:04:56.480 --> 01:04:57.320
Yeah.

1360
01:04:57.600 --> 01:05:06.580
I felt like Jim's obvious disdain for this screenplay and for any of Ryan Howard's ideas

1361
01:05:07.480 --> 01:05:11.020
made him a very, very bad agent.

1362
01:05:11.560 --> 01:05:11.760
Yeah.

1363
01:05:12.200 --> 01:05:16.020
The whole point of being an agent is to blow smoke up people's asses.

1364
01:05:16.020 --> 01:05:21.520
I mean, this is like agent thing number one is to make your client feel like the most

1365
01:05:21.520 --> 01:05:26.280
talented, beautiful, wonderful idea person.

1366
01:05:26.760 --> 01:05:27.200
Right?

1367
01:05:27.420 --> 01:05:28.180
Like, that's it.

1368
01:05:28.320 --> 01:05:32.100
Like, whatever ridiculous thing you say, they're like, oh, yeah, yeah.

1369
01:05:32.480 --> 01:05:33.600
Oh, 100%.

1370
01:05:33.600 --> 01:05:34.860
I'm right on it.

1371
01:05:34.920 --> 01:05:36.180
Like they should be a yes man?

1372
01:05:36.340 --> 01:05:36.760
A hundred.

1373
01:05:36.940 --> 01:05:37.680
They're your hype.

1374
01:05:37.780 --> 01:05:39.180
They're your main hype man.

1375
01:05:39.180 --> 01:05:40.720
Like, you know what I mean?

1376
01:05:40.860 --> 01:05:45.920
And I just felt like Jim's disdain was so obvious.

1377
01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:50.540
I actually, I really liked it how Craig was playing Daryl.

1378
01:05:50.700 --> 01:05:59.600
It was really funny to me because I felt like Daryl was clearly uncomfortable, but he was

1379
01:05:59.600 --> 01:06:03.320
also going along with it in a way that was more believable.

1380
01:06:03.320 --> 01:06:05.460
He was like, this probably will never get made.

1381
01:06:05.600 --> 01:06:05.880
Yeah.

1382
01:06:06.040 --> 01:06:07.520
But I'm going to hear this guy out.

1383
01:06:07.900 --> 01:06:08.320
Yeah.

1384
01:06:08.540 --> 01:06:13.960
Well, one of the things we asked Ryan was if he had any observations from his time on

1385
01:06:13.960 --> 01:06:14.360
set.

1386
01:06:14.520 --> 01:06:15.940
And here is what he had to say.

1387
01:06:16.340 --> 01:06:21.940
I mean, really and truthfully, I think the biggest observation for me was, was just seeing

1388
01:06:22.580 --> 01:06:26.020
you guys as the actors in your niche.

1389
01:06:26.360 --> 01:06:31.460
You know, I was always so used to people seeing me in my comfort zone, you know, playing baseball

1390
01:06:32.080 --> 01:06:38.880
and being able to watch other people like in their comfort zone and their niche of what

1391
01:06:38.880 --> 01:06:39.400
they do.

1392
01:06:39.480 --> 01:06:44.600
I always think is very, very cool and very exciting to be able to do.

1393
01:06:44.840 --> 01:06:50.540
So, I mean, my biggest observation was just kind of watching and how, how good, how fast,

1394
01:06:50.680 --> 01:06:55.760
how professional everybody was and just how much fun everybody got to have with the show.

1395
01:06:55.920 --> 01:06:59.480
I'm like, honestly, I don't know how, you know, you guys make it through half of the

1396
01:07:00.140 --> 01:07:03.580
half of the takes, you know, with just all the funny stuff.

1397
01:07:03.580 --> 01:07:06.220
You really got to kind of keep it, keep it together.

1398
01:07:06.460 --> 01:07:09.940
But, you know, I think that was one of the biggest things was just the professionalism

1399
01:07:09.940 --> 01:07:13.880
that everybody had and the food, the food behind the scenes.

1400
01:07:13.960 --> 01:07:18.060
I don't know what it is, but you guys somehow y'all might have, I mean, I guess obviously

1401
01:07:18.060 --> 01:07:22.980
having a show like the office, I mean, you get, get hooked up with some pretty good food,

1402
01:07:23.180 --> 01:07:24.080
production food back there.

1403
01:07:24.080 --> 01:07:26.900
So, the food was really, really good as well.

1404
01:07:27.040 --> 01:07:28.360
So, love that.

1405
01:07:29.660 --> 01:07:30.860
We did have good food.

1406
01:07:30.880 --> 01:07:31.140
We did.

1407
01:07:31.340 --> 01:07:34.000
It's the one thing my dad said when he left the set.

1408
01:07:34.200 --> 01:07:36.300
So many people comment on it.

1409
01:07:36.880 --> 01:07:42.900
Well, finally, we had to ask Ryan Howard what it was like having his name as a character's

1410
01:07:42.900 --> 01:07:44.120
name on the office.

1411
01:07:45.140 --> 01:07:46.760
Actually, it's been really, really cool.

1412
01:07:46.760 --> 01:07:53.960
So, it's funny because I would go to places, whether I'm going to get coffee or, you know,

1413
01:07:54.060 --> 01:07:56.860
just some other stores or things of that sort.

1414
01:07:57.840 --> 01:08:00.960
And, you know, some people are able to kind of tie it back together.

1415
01:08:01.140 --> 01:08:06.080
But even more importantly, it's like some people would look at me and they're like, man, you

1416
01:08:06.080 --> 01:08:06.740
look familiar.

1417
01:08:07.340 --> 01:08:10.720
And I would say, oh, yeah, you know, I get that every once and again.

1418
01:08:10.880 --> 01:08:13.640
It's like, you know, hey, I know what it is.

1419
01:08:14.040 --> 01:08:15.000
You were in the office.

1420
01:08:16.000 --> 01:08:17.380
And it's like, oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

1421
01:08:17.440 --> 01:08:21.580
It's like some people may have known me more from the office than actually as a baseball

1422
01:08:21.580 --> 01:08:21.960
player.

1423
01:08:21.960 --> 01:08:26.760
So, that was always something that I would, you know, get a good laugh at and must have

1424
01:08:26.760 --> 01:08:27.700
tried to do something right.

1425
01:08:27.700 --> 01:08:29.000
So, to be able to have that.

1426
01:08:29.180 --> 01:08:33.160
So, I think it's pretty cool that people could recognize me from the office and more so

1427
01:08:33.160 --> 01:08:34.280
than that from baseball.

1428
01:08:34.520 --> 01:08:37.560
But, yeah, again, loved my time there.

1429
01:08:38.540 --> 01:08:43.480
And just want to thank you guys for this opportunity to be able to hop on and be a part of the

1430
01:08:43.480 --> 01:08:43.900
podcaSt.

1431
01:08:44.400 --> 01:08:48.340
And I wish you guys the best of luck and look to hear from you guys in the future.

1432
01:08:49.400 --> 01:08:49.840
Amazing.

1433
01:08:50.100 --> 01:08:51.020
Such a nice guy.

1434
01:08:51.180 --> 01:08:51.500
I know.

1435
01:08:51.820 --> 01:08:54.200
Thank you so much, Ryan, for being on the podcaSt.

1436
01:08:54.880 --> 01:08:55.260
Yes.

1437
01:08:55.500 --> 01:08:57.200
Thank you for answering my DM.

1438
01:08:57.560 --> 01:08:57.800
I know.

1439
01:08:57.940 --> 01:08:58.960
So generous of you.

1440
01:08:59.780 --> 01:09:03.960
Now, we're going to find out that Esther actually really likes Dwight.

1441
01:09:04.260 --> 01:09:09.260
She comes up to him and Clark and says, listen, I think my father is planning a scheme in

1442
01:09:09.260 --> 01:09:13.760
which he's going to put more miles on the tractor while pocketing the profit behind

1443
01:09:13.760 --> 01:09:14.500
Dwight's back.

1444
01:09:14.540 --> 01:09:17.900
She says you need to go ask him for a deal based on miles.

1445
01:09:18.240 --> 01:09:18.700
Yeah.

1446
01:09:18.900 --> 01:09:21.840
She said, right now you're dividing it by days.

1447
01:09:22.280 --> 01:09:26.200
But he's planning to rent it out to a neighboring farm on the weekends.

1448
01:09:26.800 --> 01:09:32.260
And Dwight looks at her and says, wait, you'd be okay if I don't buy this tractor?

1449
01:09:32.560 --> 01:09:35.120
And she's like, what, did you just think I was tractor bait?

1450
01:09:35.880 --> 01:09:36.180
Yeah.

1451
01:09:36.960 --> 01:09:39.420
We got a soapbox about this scene.

1452
01:09:39.600 --> 01:09:41.460
It is from Laugh A in Minnesota.

1453
01:09:41.900 --> 01:09:44.840
I loved the soapbox and I'm so glad they wrote in.

1454
01:09:45.040 --> 01:09:46.380
Well, here's what they said.

1455
01:09:46.800 --> 01:09:51.040
Ladies, I have been waiting for this question since you have started the podcaSt.

1456
01:09:51.480 --> 01:09:56.640
I even had a reminder in my phone every week to check when I could submit a question

1457
01:09:56.640 --> 01:09:57.280
for this.

1458
01:09:58.000 --> 01:09:59.120
That is commitment.

1459
01:09:59.520 --> 01:10:04.440
When Esther tells Dwight that her father plans to rent out the tractor to the Vendor Kirk

1460
01:10:04.440 --> 01:10:07.860
Brothers on the weekends, she says that they will have the tractor for the same amount

1461
01:10:07.860 --> 01:10:12.220
of work days, but that her father will be putting on 10 times the miles.

1462
01:10:12.580 --> 01:10:18.860
In farming equipment, you do not record the miles, but rather the hours something is in

1463
01:10:18.860 --> 01:10:19.520
operation.

1464
01:10:19.820 --> 01:10:22.680
This is because tractors do more than just drive.

1465
01:10:22.980 --> 01:10:23.520
That's right.

1466
01:10:23.760 --> 01:10:29.200
They often have hydraulic lifts and power going to a secondary machine that can be operational

1467
01:10:29.200 --> 01:10:31.100
while the tractor is standing still.

1468
01:10:31.100 --> 01:10:33.520
It killed me when Esther said miles.

1469
01:10:33.740 --> 01:10:37.900
I knew that any good farmer would know that you got to watch the hours on an engine.

1470
01:10:38.740 --> 01:10:39.080
Mm-hmm.

1471
01:10:40.200 --> 01:10:41.080
Good catch.

1472
01:10:41.380 --> 01:10:42.740
I loved that catch.

1473
01:10:42.920 --> 01:10:46.940
And I just appreciate you setting that timer on your phone.

1474
01:10:46.940 --> 01:10:48.220
I know, all this time.

1475
01:10:48.700 --> 01:10:50.120
I would not have known this.

1476
01:10:50.220 --> 01:10:51.380
Angela, you knew this.

1477
01:10:51.580 --> 01:10:52.480
I did know this.

1478
01:10:52.600 --> 01:10:54.340
You know, I'd sort of forgotten it.

1479
01:10:54.400 --> 01:10:58.060
So I think when I watched the episode, it didn't register to me for a second.

1480
01:10:58.060 --> 01:11:01.540
But as soon as I read The Soapbox, I was like, oh, my God, yes.

1481
01:11:01.840 --> 01:11:02.040
Yeah.

1482
01:11:02.160 --> 01:11:02.380
Yes.

